\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Atayal (ISO 639-3 tay; Glottocode atay1247) is an endangered Austronesian language of Taiwan. Search results mention school teaching, including extracurricular lessons in Wulai from 1990 and teaching materials developed for specific dialects (S'uli, Matu'uwal). This differs from the reference note that it is not known to be taught in schools.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item NTHU master's thesis on developing a first-grade S'uli Atayal textbook in Taian, Miaoli: \url{https://etd.lib.nthu.edu.tw/detail/96f027f7a0022771614c8c99fd86b3df}
\item NTHU thesis analyzing the grammar of the reading and writing chapter of the Matu'uwal (Wenshui) Atayal course materials: \url{https://etd.lib.nthu.edu.tw/detail/22c79aea6e7bb240468f576ddd584e22}
\end{itemize}
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Omniglot, Atayal writing and language (mentions an Atayal-English dictionary and grammar books, without full citations): \url{https://www.omniglot.com/writing/atayal.htm}
\item Taiwan National Academy for Educational Research article on Atayal narratives and culturally responsive curriculum: \url{https://ej.naer.edu.tw/JTR/ccount/click.php?id=357}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in searches. A news report on elderly Kayu Village residents learning the Latin alphabet to teach grandchildren appeared: \url{https://news.ltn.com.tw/news/focus/breakingnews/2868933}. I did not verify its contents.
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found beyond the above.
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Teaching materials exist at least in dialect-specific form, and romanization was standardized in 2005 according to search summaries. Dictionary and Ministry of Education or Council of Indigenous Peoples textbook titles were not located and should be checked directly.
\end{document}
